Z||000000||bHooper||10-31-1999||01:37 PM||||I have started doing a "mild" restoration of my Gt and have noticed rust in a few places.  Such as the subframe supports for the front and rear fascia's, as well as the plate underneath the radiator.  this is all a surface rust situation, does anyone have any good tips for removeing this?  I have brushed it down and applied navel jelly, however it came back.   Instead, should I apply a rust inhibitor such as a krylon product (after i brush it to the metal)?  Help!!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||10-31-1999||04:01 PM||||Try rust converter. It turns the rust black and it also covers the treated area with a protective coating-the stuff actually works||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||10-31-1999||04:55 PM||||The Eastwood company has everything your looking for; <A HREF="http://www.eastwoodcompany.com" TARGET=_blank>www.eastwoodcompany.com</A>  They sell a rust neutralizing paint called Corroless that is what your looking for.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gokart||10-31-1999||05:48 PM||||I'd go with Phil! I did the entire under carriage with rust preventor that turns rust into a black primer, then went over that with 3 cans of rubberized undercoating.||||reg||14
Z||000004||bHooper||10-31-1999||05:51 PM||||I've seen what you are talking about, i always thought it was a "gimic", and i will try it.  Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000005||2M4 Dale||10-31-1999||06:58 PM||||Try Penetrol, it really works good, you can put it on straight or mix it with paint. They have a toll free number fot more info. It is 800/321-3444.||||reg||14
